+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+-   **Dependent Texture Fetches:** The ATI 9700 has a limited number of dependent texture fetches. The algorithm must minimize these to avoid performance degradation [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Floating Point Textures:** Accumulation textures must support floating point values to handle negative values and high dynamic range, which are available on the target hardware [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Early Z-Test Dependency:** The acceleration relies on the early z-test being enabled and functioning correctly. If the hardware does not support early z-test or depth replace, the method may not provide the expected performance gains [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $P$ is the number of main passes. The early z-test reduces the effective number of passes for opaque regions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \cdot H)$ for the accumulation textures, where $W$ and $H$ are the viewport dimensions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Performance gains up to a factor of 3 for typical renditions of volumetric data sets on the ATI 9700 graphics card [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Exploits intrinsic parallelism of fragment processors; efficient communication between passes via texture render targets; effective early ray termination via early z-test [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited by the number of dependent texture fetches on the hardware; fixed number of passes may lead to unnecessary computations for empty space if not combined with empty-space skipping [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When targeting DirectX 9-level hardware with programmable pixel shaders and the need for early ray termination [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When targeting hardware with limited dependent texture fetches or when more advanced acceleration techniques like empty-space skipping are required [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $S$ is the number of steps (passes). Early termination reduces the effective number of steps for opaque regions [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for accumulation textures and ray direction textures, plus $O(V)$ for volume data, where $V$ is the volume size [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Measured Performance:** Performance gains up to a factor of 3 were demonstrated on the ATI 9700 graphics card for typical volumetric data sets [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Strengths:** Exploits hardware parallelism; enables early ray termination and empty-space skipping; efficient communication between passes via texture render targets [S1, unofficial].
+-   **Limitations:** Requires specific hardware features (Pixel Shader 2.0, depth replace, texture render targets); limited dependent texture fetches on ATI hardware; fixed number of steps may lead to over-sampling in empty space if not optimized [S1, unofficial].
+-   **When to Use:** Real-time volume rendering on DirectX 9-level hardware where interactivity is required [S1, unofficial].
+-   **When Not to Use:** On hardware lacking the required features (e.g., no depth replace or texture render targets); for offline rendering where higher quality techniques (e.g., path tracing) are acceptable [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method was proposed in 2003 by researchers at the Technical University of Munich (TUM) [S1, unofficial]. It was designed to integrate acceleration techniques into 3D texture-based volume rendering on programmable graphics hardware, specifically targeting the capabilities of DirectX 9-level hardware like the ATI 9700 [S1, unofficial]. The method builds on the observation that CPU-based volume rendering is insufficient for real-time interaction with large data sets, and leverages the parallelism of GPU fragment processors [S1, unofficial].
+The method was introduced in 2003 by researchers at the Technical University of Munich (TUM) in the paper "Acceleration Techniques for GPU-based Volume Rendering" [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the challenge of real-time volume rendering on large data sets, which was not feasible on general-purpose CPUs at the time [S1, unofficial]. The work built upon the capabilities of programmable graphics hardware, specifically the Pixel Shader 2.0 API available in DirectX 9-level hardware like the ATI 9700 [S1, unofficial]. The "stream model" concept was proposed to effectively integrate acceleration techniques like early ray termination into 3D texture-based volume rendering by exploiting the intrinsic parallelism and efficient communication on modern graphics chips [S1, unofficial].
 
